"""
fil_nand_emu — Vt-physics NAND backend (opt-in alternate to fil_nand_device).

Where `fil_nand_device` drives a *behavioural* ONFI chip (one i64 per page,
status flags synthesised directly), this backend stores the page's main byte in
a real threshold-voltage cell array — the `hardware.nand_emu` NeChip emulator
(profile S1) — and drives it through the SAME pin-level command / address / data
handshake:

    READ    :  00h , <col,row> , 30h , (data_out streams the page register)
    PROGRAM :  80h , <col,row> , (data_in) , 10h
    ERASE   :  60h , <row>     , D0h
    STATUS  :  70h            (read status register — I/O0 = FAIL)

Because the byte now lives in physical cells, a page read reflects the analog Vt
after lazy retention / read-disturb drift: an aged, worn block reads back with
real bit flips at the default read reference and reads clean once the reference
is shifted (read-retry) — the FTL-visible failure the Vt model exists to produce.
This is the payoff over `fil_nand_device`, whose bytes never decay.

Drop-in contract: `NandEmu` exposes the exact same public seam as
`fil_nand_device.NandDevice` / `fil_nand.Nand`
(`program / read_page / erase_block / erase_count / inject_prog_fail /
inject_erase_fail / inject_read_bitflip / corrupt_page_data / set_bad`),
returning the shared `fil_nand.NandRead`. It is an API-compatible drop-in,
verified at the device level (fil_nand_emu_check.spl) AND end to end through
fil/ftl (fil_nand_emu_e2e_check.spl).

It is not the DEFAULT backend — `fil_new()` / `ftl_new()` still build
`NandDevice` — but a backend-selection seam does exist and this backend is
selected explicitly through it by a test that wants the physics:
  `ftl.spl:ftl_new_emu()` -> `fil.spl:fil_new_emu()` -> `fil_fmc.spl:fmc_new_emu()`
Callers today: fil_nand_emu_e2e_check, rel_ladder_check, rel_wiring_check,
rel_seams_check.

Two honest divergences from `fil_nand_device`, both because this backend is
*real-NAND faithful* rather than a placeholder:
  1. An erased / never-programmed page reads FFh (the real NAND erased state),
     not 0 — `fil_nand_device` returns a placeholder 0. OOB still reads UNMAP.
  2. The `err` field carries a PHYSICS-derived bit-error count — popcount(sensed
     XOR last-written byte, via the per-page `shadow`) — with any injected flips
     added on top, where `fil_nand_device` reports only the injected count. So
     retention / read-disturb drift surfaces a real corrected-bit count (not just
     wrong data): a drooped single cell reads back as an ECC-correctable single-bit
     error, a heavily-drifted page as an honest uncorrectable one. Consequence: a
     `corrupt_page_data` overwrite is counted the same way (sensed diverges from
     the written intent), so it is caught via the bit-error path — a 3+bit silent
     corrupt now correctly FAILs instead of risking a syndrome mis-correction.

Geometry — RECONCILED, and checked at construction (was a silent divergence,
doc/08_tracking/bug/payload_widening_did_not_reach_emu_backend_2026-09-01.md §2):

    firmware page  =  PAGE_BYTES 4096  +  OOB_BYTES 128  =  4224 bytes
    NeProfile.S3   =  page_bytes 4224  (8 x 528-byte sectors, spare included)
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    4096 + 128 == 4224  ->  S3 is the EXACT fit, so the profile is S3.

S1/S2 (528 B) cannot hold a 4096-byte page at all; that is why this backend used
to store one byte. `emu_geometry_ok` asserts the identity above at construction
and `nand_emu_new` fails closed (returns a device whose every op is a bad block)
rather than silently narrowing if the profile is ever changed.

Column map inside the 4224-byte page:
    columns    0 .. 4095   main area — PageData word i occupies columns 8i..8i+7,
                           little-endian (byte k of word i at column 8i+k)
    columns 4096 .. 4223   spare area — left erased (FFh). OOB (lba / seq / ecc)
                           stays in the side arrays below, exactly as
                           fil_nand_device keeps it out of `page_data`.

Address shrink: the firmware addresses a 64-block × 64-page space (nvme_types)
while S3 backs 16 blocks × 8 pages. Firmware addresses fold (block mod 16, page
mod 8); distinct (block<16, page<8) addresses map 1:1 and round-trip exactly.
Addresses beyond the window alias — the documented shrink behaviour (doc §4).

Payload width: `program_page` / `read_page_data` carry a REAL 4096-byte PageData
through the pin handshake (4096 data_in / data_out cycles), so every column is
backed by Vt cells and wear / retention / read-retry act on the whole page. The
scalar `program` / `read_page.data` seam is deliberately UNCHANGED — it still
programs and senses column 0 only, leaving the rest of the page erased — because
the FIL scalar ECC and every existing emu check are written against that byte.

Test seam (for FTL fault-injection tests): `inject` / `advance_time_s` /
`set_vref_offset` forward to the NeChip, and `set_block_wear` seeds a block's
program/erase-cycle count so a "worn" block can be modelled without simulating
thousands of real erases (pe_cycles IS the emulator's entire wear state, so
seeding it is identical to having cycled the block that many times).
"""

use nvme_types.*
use nvme_payload.*   # PageData + PAGE_BYTES / PAGE_WORDS / OOB_BYTES
use fil_nand.*   # NandRead (shared FIL read result {data, lba, seq, ecc, err})
use fil_ecc.*
use std.hardware.nand_emu.chip.{NeChip, ne_chip_new}
use std.hardware.nand_emu.nand_types.{NeProfile, NeGeometry, NeFault}
use std.hardware.nand_emu.nand_types.{ne_erased_byte, ne_vt_prog_mean, ne_vt_erase_mean}
use std.hardware.nand_emu.geometry.ne_cell_index
use std.hardware.nand_emu.vt_array.{ne_block_meta_get, ne_block_meta_set}

# ── ONFI opcodes + status bit (mirror fil_nand_device / doc §1.3, §1.4) ────────
val EMU_READ_1ST:  i64 = 0x00
val EMU_READ_2ND:  i64 = 0x30
val EMU_PROG_1ST:  i64 = 0x80
val EMU_PROG_2ND:  i64 = 0x10
val EMU_ERASE_1ST: i64 = 0x60
val EMU_ERASE_2ND: i64 = 0xD0
val EMU_STATUS_FAIL: i64 = 0x01   # 70h I/O0 = 1 on failure

# ── Address shrink: fold a firmware ppn/blk into the S1 window ─────────────────
fn emu_fold_row(fw_ppn: i64, geo: NeGeometry) -> i64:
    """Fold a firmware page number (0..NUM_PAGES) into an S1 row (0..total_pages):
    block mod S1-blocks, page mod S1-pages-per-block. 1:1 for block<8, page<4."""
    val blk: i64 = ppn_block(fw_ppn)
    val page: i64 = ppn_page(fw_ppn)
    val ne_blk: i64 = blk % geo.blocks
    val ne_page: i64 = page % geo.pages_per_block
    ne_blk * geo.pages_per_block + ne_page

fn emu_fold_blk(fw_blk: i64, geo: NeGeometry) -> i64:
    """Fold a firmware block index into the S1 block window (block mod S1-blocks)."""
    fw_blk % geo.blocks

fn emu_first_row_of_blk(ne_blk: i64, geo: NeGeometry) -> i64:
    """First S1 row of an S1 block (its lowest page)."""
    ne_blk * geo.pages_per_block

fn emu_erase_counter_next(v: i64) -> i64:
    """Saturating erase-count increment (matches nand_device_erase_counter_next)."""
    if v < 0:
        return 1
    if v >= FW_SAFE_I64_LIMIT:
        return FW_SAFE_I64_LIMIT
    v + 1

fn emu_popcount8(v: i64) -> i64:
    """Number of set bits in the low 8 bits of `v` (0..8). Used to derive the
    physics bit-error count of a read as popcount(sensed XOR last-written byte)."""
    var x: i64 = v & 0xFF
    var count: i64 = 0
    var bit: i64 = 0
    while bit < 8:
        count = count + ((x >> bit) & 1)
        bit = bit + 1
    count

# ── Pin-level address latching (return-the-object; chip is a value type) ───────
fn emu_latch_addr5(chip: NeChip, row: i64) -> NeChip:
    """Drive the 5-cycle READ/PROGRAM address (column 0, the given row) onto the
    ALE pin, one byte per cycle (doc §1.2 cycle map)."""
    var c = chip
    c = c.addr(0)                    # cyc1: column A0-A7  = 0
    c = c.addr(0)                    # cyc2: column A8-A12 = 0
    c = c.addr(row & 0xFF)           # cyc3: row A13-A20
    c = c.addr((row >> 8) & 0xFF)    # cyc4: row A21-A28
    c = c.addr((row >> 16) & 0x3)    # cyc5: row A29-A30
    c

fn emu_latch_addr3(chip: NeChip, row: i64) -> NeChip:
    """Drive the 3-cycle ERASE address (row only) onto the ALE pin (doc §1.2)."""
    var c = chip
    c = c.addr(row & 0xFF)
    c = c.addr((row >> 8) & 0xFF)
    c = c.addr((row >> 16) & 0x3)
    c

fn emu_geometry_ok(geo: NeGeometry) -> bool:
    """The checked page-geometry identity: the emulated page must hold the whole
    firmware page plus its spare area, i.e. page_bytes == PAGE_BYTES + OOB_BYTES
    (4224 == 4096 + 128, NeProfile.S3). False for S1/S2 (528 B), which physically
    cannot store a 4096-byte page."""
    geo.page_bytes == PAGE_BYTES + OOB_BYTES

fn emu_page_byte(p: PageData, col: i64) -> i64:
    """Byte at main-area column `col` of a page: byte (col mod 8) of word col/8,
    little-endian."""
    val w: i64 = page_word(p, col / 8)
    (w >> ((col % 8) * 8)) & 0xFF

fn emu_bytes_to_page(bytes: [i64]) -> PageData:
    """Assemble PAGE_WORDS little-endian words from PAGE_BYTES sensed bytes."""
    var w: [i64] = []
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < PAGE_WORDS:
        var acc: i64 = 0
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 8:
            acc = acc | ((bytes[i * 8 + k] & 0xFF) << (k * 8))
            k = k + 1
        w.push(acc)
        i = i + 1
    PageData(words: w)

fn emu_page_erased() -> PageData:
    """The intent page of a never-programmed (erased) page: every main-area byte
    FFh. Words are all-ones, so `emu_page_byte` yields 0xFF at every column —
    the page-wide generalisation of the old scalar `shadow` preset 0xFF."""
    page_fill(-1)

fn emu_page_bit_errors(sensed: PageData, intent: PageData) -> i64:
    """Raw bit-error count over the WHOLE main area: sum over all PAGE_BYTES
    columns of popcount(sensed XOR intent). The page-wide generalisation of the
    old column-0-only `popcount8(sensed ^ shadow)`."""
    var total: i64 = 0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < PAGE_WORDS:
        val x: i64 = page_word(sensed, i) ^ page_word(intent, i)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 8:
            total = total + emu_popcount8(x >> (k * 8))
            k = k + 1
        i = i + 1
    total

fn emu_codeword_bit_errors(sensed: PageData, intent: PageData, cw: i64) -> i64:
    """Raw bit-error count inside ONE 1024-byte ECC codeword. This is the unit a
    real controller reports corrected-bit telemetry in, and the unit SEC-DED can
    actually act on: one bad bit per codeword is correctable even when four
    codewords each carry one, which a page-summed count could never express."""
    val s: CodewordSpan = codeword_span(cw)
    var total: i64 = 0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < s.word_count:
        val x: i64 = page_word(sensed, s.page_word_off + i) ^ page_word(intent, s.page_word_off + i)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 8:
            total = total + emu_popcount8(x >> (k * 8))
            k = k + 1
        i = i + 1
    total

# One sense, reported at three widths. `rd` is the unchanged scalar NandRead seam
# (column-0 byte + OOB + the column-0 `err`); `page` is the real 4096-byte main
# area; `err_page` and `err_cw_max` are the PAGE-WIDE error telemetry that the
# column-0 `err` alone could not express.
struct EmuSense:
    page:       PageData
    rd:         NandRead
    err_page:   i64   # raw bit errors across the whole main area
    err_cw_max: i64   # raw bit errors in the WORST 1024-byte ECC codeword

# ── NandEmu: the physics-backed drop-in device ────────────────────────────────
struct NandEmu:
    """A NAND device backed by the Vt-physics NeChip emulator. Composition, not
    inheritance: the analog media is the NeChip; OOB metadata, the no-overwrite
    guard, bad-block / fault flags and erase counters are kept alongside it (same
    role they play in fil_nand_device), all indexed by the folded S1 row/block."""
    chip: NeChip
    oob_lba:    [i64]   # S1 total_pages, spare-area logical addr (UNMAP if unwritten)
    oob_seq:    [i64]   # S1 total_pages, spare-area write sequence number
    oob_ecc:    [i64]   # S1 total_pages, spare-area ECC stored at program time
    programmed: [i64]   # S1 total_pages, 1 = page holds data (no overwrite w/o erase)
    injected:   [i64]   # S1 total_pages, injected read bit-flip count (adds to err)
    shadow:     [i64]   # total_pages, last-written COLUMN-0 byte (0xFF = erased).
                        #   Kept as the SCALAR seam feeding NandRead.err, which the
                        #   landed byte-scoped oracles (rel_seams_check "exactly ONE
                        #   programmed cell in column 0") are written against.
    shadow_page: [PageData]  # total_pages, last-written INTENT for the WHOLE main
                        #   area (erased = all-FFh). `sense_read` derives the
                        #   page-wide bit-error count as popcount(sensed XOR this)
                        #   over all PAGE_BYTES columns, and the per-codeword count
                        #   SEC-DED can actually act on. This is the widening the
                        #   old note here deferred: `err` reporting is no longer
                        #   column-0 scoped, only `NandRead.err` is (deliberately).
    oob_par:    [i64]   # total_pages * CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE, the REAL per-codeword
                        #   SEC-DED parity (fil_ecc ecc_page_compute) stored at
                        #   program_page time — the emu's equivalent of
                        #   NandDevice.read_page_parity, so the widened ECC decode
                        #   in fil.decode_read runs on this backend too.
    par_valid:  [i64]   # total_pages, 1 = oob_par holds a real widened parity set.
                        #   A page written through the SCALAR program path has none
                        #   (only column 0 was driven), so it stays 0 and the
                        #   widened decode fails closed to the legacy verdict.
    bad_block:  [i64]   # S1 blocks, 1 = block retired
    blk_erase:  [i64]   # S1 blocks, cumulative erase count
    prog_fail:  [i64]   # S1 blocks, 1 = next program to this block fails (one-shot)
    erase_fail: [i64]   # S1 blocks, 1 = next erase of this block fails (one-shot)

fn nand_emu_new() -> NandEmu:
    """Construct a fresh S1-backed emulated device: an idle NeChip plus empty OOB
    and per-block state. The Vt array itself is allocated lazily by the NeChip on
    its first operation (erased distribution), so a never-programmed page reads FFh."""
    val chip = ne_chip_new(NeProfile.S3)
    val geo = chip.geometry
    # Fail closed rather than narrow silently: if the profile ever stops matching
    # PAGE_BYTES + OOB_BYTES the device is built with zero backed pages/blocks, so
    # every op returns NAND_BAD_BLOCK instead of storing a truncated page.
    var backed_pages:  i64 = geo.total_pages
    var backed_blocks: i64 = geo.blocks
    if not emu_geometry_ok(geo):
        backed_pages = 0
        backed_blocks = 0
    var oob_lba:    [i64] = []
    var oob_seq:    [i64] = []
    var oob_ecc:    [i64] = []
    var programmed: [i64] = []
    var injected:   [i64] = []
    var shadow:     [i64] = []
    var shadow_page: [PageData] = []
    var oob_par:    [i64] = []
    var par_valid:  [i64] = []
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < backed_pages:
        oob_lba.push(UNMAP)
        oob_seq.push(0)
        oob_ecc.push(0)
        programmed.push(0)
        injected.push(0)
        shadow.push(0xFF)          # erased pages sense FFh -> zero physics error
        shadow_page.push(emu_page_erased())
        par_valid.push(0)
        var pi: i64 = 0
        while pi < CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE:
            oob_par.push(0)
            pi = pi + 1
        i = i + 1

    var bad_block:  [i64] = []
    var blk_erase:  [i64] = []
    var prog_fail:  [i64] = []
    var erase_fail: [i64] = []
    i = 0
    while i < backed_blocks:
        bad_block.push(0)
        blk_erase.push(0)
        prog_fail.push(0)
        erase_fail.push(0)
        i = i + 1

    NandEmu(chip: chip, oob_lba: oob_lba, oob_seq: oob_seq, oob_ecc: oob_ecc, programmed: programmed, injected: injected, shadow: shadow, shadow_page: shadow_page, oob_par: oob_par, par_valid: par_valid, bad_block: bad_block, blk_erase: blk_erase, prog_fail: prog_fail, erase_fail: erase_fail)

impl NandEmu:
    # ── High-level FIL seam (drop-in for fil_nand_device.NandDevice) ───────────
    me program(ppn: i64, lba: i64, seq: i64, data: i64) -> i64:
        """Program one page (main byte + OOB) via the ONFI pin handshake. Returns
        NAND_OK / NAND_BAD_BLOCK / NAND_PROG_FAIL, matching fil_nand_device: bad
        block and one-shot program faults are rejected up front, an already-written
        page is refused (NAND cannot overwrite without an erase), and a NeChip-level
        program failure (an armed ProgramFail fault) surfaces as the 70h FAIL bit."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        val ne_blk: i64 = row / geo.pages_per_block
        if ne_blk >= me.bad_block.len():
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.bad_block[ne_blk] != 0:
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.prog_fail[ne_blk] != 0:
            me.prog_fail[ne_blk] = 0
            return NAND_PROG_FAIL
        if row >= me.programmed.len():
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.programmed[row] != 0:
            return NAND_PROG_FAIL
        # Pin sequence: 80h , <col=0,row> , data_in , 10h .
        var c = me.chip
        c = c.cmd(EMU_PROG_1ST)          # presets page register to FFh, opens load
        c = emu_latch_addr5(c, row)      # -> ProgLoad, cur_row = row, col_counter = 0
        c = c.data_in(data & 0xFF)       # main byte into column 0
        c = c.cmd(EMU_PROG_2ND)          # execute (MODEL busy) -> status.pass set
        me.chip = c
        if (me.chip.read_status() & EMU_STATUS_FAIL) == EMU_STATUS_FAIL:
            return NAND_PROG_FAIL
        me.oob_lba[row] = lba
        me.oob_seq[row] = seq
        me.oob_ecc[row] = ecc_compute(data, lba, seq)
        me.programmed[row] = 1
        me.shadow[row] = data & 0xFF     # record intent for the physics err count
        # Page-wide intent for the SCALAR path: only column 0 was driven, so every
        # other column is still at the erased FFh preset. Recorded truthfully so
        # the page-wide err count of a scalar-programmed page is 0, not 4095 bytes
        # of phantom error. No widened parity is stored (par_valid stays 0) —
        # nothing computed parity over columns this path never wrote.
        var sp: PageData = emu_page_erased()
        sp.words[0] = (sp.words[0] & (0 - 256)) | (data & 0xFF)
        me.shadow_page[row] = sp
        me.par_valid[row] = 0
        NAND_OK

    me program_page(ppn: i64, lba: i64, seq: i64, data: PageData) -> i64:
        """Program a REAL 4096-byte page (main area + OOB) via the ONFI pin
        handshake — the widened seam, drop-in for fil_nand_device.program_page.
        Identical guard order and return codes to the scalar `program` above; the
        only difference is that all PAGE_BYTES columns are driven through data_in
        (word i little-endian at columns 8i..8i+7) instead of column 0 alone, so
        every payload bit is backed by real Vt cells. The spare columns
        (PAGE_BYTES..page_bytes) are left at the FFh preset — OOB lives in the side
        arrays, exactly as fil_nand_device keeps it out of `page_data`.
        The stored ECC stays the fil_ecc scalar over the COLUMN-0 byte, matching
        what `read_page` returns as `data`; page-wide integrity is carried by the
        page-wide bit-error count `err`, not by this scalar."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        if not emu_geometry_ok(geo):
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        val ne_blk: i64 = row / geo.pages_per_block
        if ne_blk >= me.bad_block.len():
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.bad_block[ne_blk] != 0:
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.prog_fail[ne_blk] != 0:
            me.prog_fail[ne_blk] = 0
            return NAND_PROG_FAIL
        if row >= me.programmed.len():
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.programmed[row] != 0:
            return NAND_PROG_FAIL
        var c = me.chip
        c = c.cmd(EMU_PROG_1ST)          # presets page register to FFh, opens load
        c = emu_latch_addr5(c, row)      # -> ProgLoad, cur_row = row, col_counter = 0
        var col: i64 = 0
        while col < PAGE_BYTES:
            c = c.data_in(emu_page_byte(data, col))
            col = col + 1
        c = c.cmd(EMU_PROG_2ND)          # execute (MODEL busy) -> status.pass set
        me.chip = c
        if (me.chip.read_status() & EMU_STATUS_FAIL) == EMU_STATUS_FAIL:
            return NAND_PROG_FAIL
        val byte0: i64 = emu_page_byte(data, 0)
        me.oob_lba[row] = lba
        me.oob_seq[row] = seq
        me.oob_ecc[row] = ecc_compute(byte0, lba, seq)
        me.programmed[row] = 1
        me.shadow[row] = byte0
        # Page-wide intent: ecc_page_clone, never `= data`, because on the current
        # Rust seed `var q: PageData = p` ALIASES the nested words array
        # (doc/08_tracking/bug/struct_nested_array_assign_aliases_not_cow_2026-09-01.md)
        # and the caller's page would then be mutated by any later media write.
        me.shadow_page[row] = ecc_page_clone(data)
        # REAL per-codeword SEC-DED parity over all 512 words — the same
        # ecc_page_compute the behavioural NandDevice backend stores. This is what
        # lets fil.decode_read run its widened decode on the emu backend.
        val par: [i64] = ecc_page_compute(data, lba, seq)
        var ci: i64 = 0
        while ci < CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE:
            me.oob_par[row * CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE + ci] = par[ci]
            ci = ci + 1
        me.par_valid[row] = 1
        NAND_OK

    me sense_page(ppn: i64) -> PageData:
        """Sense the whole main area of a page through the ONFI handshake and
        reassemble it into a PageData: 00h , <col 0,row> , 30h , then PAGE_BYTES
        data_out cycles. Every returned bit comes out of the Vt cells, so retention
        / read-disturb drift and the current Vref offset apply to the whole page.
        Mutating (`me`): a sense advances the emulated clock and materialises lazy
        drift, exactly as read_page does."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return page_zero()
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        if not emu_geometry_ok(geo):
            return page_zero()
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        var c = me.chip
        c = c.cmd(EMU_READ_1ST)
        c = emu_latch_addr5(c, row)
        c = c.cmd(EMU_READ_2ND)
        var bytes: [i64] = []
        var col: i64 = 0
        while col < PAGE_BYTES:
            bytes.push(c.data_out())
            col = col + 1
        me.chip = c
        emu_bytes_to_page(bytes)

    me read_page_data(ppn: i64) -> PageData:
        """The widened read seam (drop-in for fil_nand_device.read_page_data): the
        REAL 4096-byte main area as sensed from the cells. An out-of-range or
        unbacked page returns the zero placeholder the behavioural backend uses."""
        me.sense_page(ppn)

    me read_page(ppn: i64) -> NandRead:
        """Read one page via the ONFI handshake. `data` is the byte sensed from the
        Vt cells at column 0 (so it reflects retention / disturb drift); OOB and the
        stored ECC come from the spare area; `err` is the injected bit-flip count.
        An out-of-range page reports an uncorrectable read (err = 2), as
        fil_nand_device does; a never-programmed page senses FFh (erased)."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return NandRead(data: 0, lba: UNMAP, seq: 0, ecc: 0, err: 2)
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        # Pin sequence: 00h , <col=0,row> , 30h , data_out . Only column 0 is
        # streamed here: this is the SCALAR seam (see the `shadow` field note).
        # The widened seam is read_page_data, which streams all PAGE_BYTES columns.
        var c = me.chip
        c = c.cmd(EMU_READ_1ST)
        c = emu_latch_addr5(c, row)      # -> AddrLatch(00h,5), cur_row = row, col 0
        c = c.cmd(EMU_READ_2ND)          # sense page into the register (MODEL busy)
        val databyte: i64 = c.data_out() # stream column 0 (advances the column counter)
        me.chip = c
        # Physics-derived bit-error count: how many bits of the sensed byte differ
        # from the last-written payload (retention / read-disturb drift makes drooped
        # programmed cells sense 1). This models the corrected-bit telemetry a real
        # ECC decoder reports for a page — NOT a claim that hardware compares against
        # written data. Injected flips (fault-injection) ADD to it, so a test can
        # stack an artificial count on top of real drift.
        var inj: i64 = me.injected[row]
        if inj < 0:
            inj = 0
        val phys: i64 = emu_popcount8(databyte ^ (me.shadow[row] & 0xFF))
        val err: i64 = phys + inj
        NandRead(data: databyte, lba: me.oob_lba[row], seq: me.oob_seq[row], ecc: me.oob_ecc[row], err: err)

    me read_page_parity(ppn: i64) -> [i64]:
        """The widened per-codeword SEC-DED parity stored for this page, or an
        EMPTY array when this page carries none (never programmed, erased, or
        written through the scalar column-0 program path). Drop-in for
        fil_nand_device.read_page_parity, so `fil.decode_read` can run the same
        widened ECC decode on the Vt-physics backend as on the behavioural one.

        Fails CLOSED by returning []: ecc_page_decode treats a short parity set as
        uncorrectable, and fil.decode_read skips the widened decode entirely, so an
        absent parity set can never be mistaken for a page that verified."""
        var none: [i64] = []
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return none
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        if not emu_geometry_ok(geo):
            return none
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        if row >= me.par_valid.len():
            return none
        if me.par_valid[row] == 0:
            return none
        var out: [i64] = []
        var i: i64 = 0
        while i < CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE:
            out.push(me.oob_par[row * CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE + i])
            i = i + 1
        out

    me sense_read(ppn: i64) -> EmuSense:
        """ONE sense of the page, reported at three widths.

        This replaces the read path's previous TWO independent senses
        (`read_page` for the scalar seam + `read_page_data` for the page), which
        advanced the emulated clock twice per logical read and could in principle
        disagree with each other. Here `rd.data` is literally word 0 of the same
        sensed `page`, so the scalar and widened views cannot drift.

        Error telemetry, no longer column-0 scoped:
          * `rd.err`      — popcount(sensed XOR intent) at COLUMN 0 only. Kept
                            byte-scoped on purpose: it feeds the scalar fil_ecc
                            decoder, whose oracle is a single column-0 cell.
          * `err_page`    — the same popcount over ALL 4096 main-area columns.
          * `err_cw_max`  — the worst single 1024-byte ECC CODEWORD's count. This,
                            not the page sum, is the scalar SEC-DED can act on:
                            four codewords each carrying one bad bit are all
                            correctable, while two bad bits in ONE codeword are
                            not. Feeding the page SUM to ecc_page_decode would
                            declare that first, fully-recoverable page dead.
        Injected fault-injection flips are modelled at column 0 (that is where
        `inject_read_flips` acts), so they add to codeword 0 and to the page total.
        """
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return EmuSense(page: page_zero(), rd: NandRead(data: 0, lba: UNMAP, seq: 0, ecc: 0, err: 2), err_page: 0, err_cw_max: 2)
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        if not emu_geometry_ok(geo):
            return EmuSense(page: page_zero(), rd: NandRead(data: 0, lba: UNMAP, seq: 0, ecc: 0, err: 2), err_page: 0, err_cw_max: 2)
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        val sensed: PageData = me.sense_page(ppn)
        val intent: PageData = me.shadow_page[row]
        var inj: i64 = me.injected[row]
        if inj < 0:
            inj = 0
        val databyte: i64 = emu_page_byte(sensed, 0)
        val err0: i64 = emu_popcount8(databyte ^ (me.shadow[row] & 0xFF)) + inj
        val ep: i64 = emu_page_bit_errors(sensed, intent) + inj
        var cwmax: i64 = 0
        var ci: i64 = 0
        while ci < CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE:
            var c: i64 = emu_codeword_bit_errors(sensed, intent, ci)
            if ci == 0:
                c = c + inj
            if c > cwmax:
                cwmax = c
            ci = ci + 1
        EmuSense(page: sensed, rd: NandRead(data: databyte, lba: me.oob_lba[row], seq: me.oob_seq[row], ecc: me.oob_ecc[row], err: err0), err_page: ep, err_cw_max: cwmax)

    me read_at_vref(ppn: i64, offset: i64) -> NandRead:
        """Read one page at a shifted read reference (read-retry ladder primitive).
        Captures the chip's current Vref offset, applies `offset` (chip-global:
        vref = 128 + offset), senses at that reference, then RESTORES the prior
        offset before returning — set→read→restore is atomic within the call so no
        shifted reference leaks to a later read. The sense still materialises lazy
        retention drift and advances the emulated clock exactly as read_page does
        (only the comparison threshold moves), so a drooped programmed cell that
        reads 1 at the default reference reads 0 again here while stored Vt / clock
        stay consistent. Out-of-range folds to an uncorrectable read (err = 2)."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return NandRead(data: 0, lba: UNMAP, seq: 0, ecc: 0, err: 2)
        val prior: i64 = me.chip.vref_offset
        me.chip = me.chip.set_vref_offset(offset)
        val rd: NandRead = me.read_page(ppn)
        me.chip = me.chip.set_vref_offset(prior)
        rd

    me erase_block(blk: i64) -> i64:
        """Erase one block via the ONFI handshake (60h , <row> , D0h). Returns
        NAND_OK / NAND_BAD_BLOCK / NAND_ERASE_FAIL. On success the four folded pages
        return to the erased state (OOB -> UNMAP, cells -> FFh) and the erase count
        bumps; a bad block or an armed one-shot erase fault is rejected."""
        if not block_in_range(blk):
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        val ne_blk: i64 = emu_fold_blk(blk, geo)
        if ne_blk >= me.bad_block.len():
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.bad_block[ne_blk] != 0:
            return NAND_BAD_BLOCK
        if me.erase_fail[ne_blk] != 0:
            me.erase_fail[ne_blk] = 0
            return NAND_ERASE_FAIL
        val first: i64 = emu_first_row_of_blk(ne_blk, geo)
        var c = me.chip
        c = c.cmd(EMU_ERASE_1ST)
        c = emu_latch_addr3(c, first)    # -> EraseSetup, cur_row in ne_blk
        c = c.cmd(EMU_ERASE_2ND)         # execute erase (MODEL busy)
        me.chip = c
        if (me.chip.read_status() & EMU_STATUS_FAIL) == EMU_STATUS_FAIL:
            return NAND_ERASE_FAIL
        var pg: i64 = 0
        while pg < geo.pages_per_block:
            val r: i64 = first + pg
            me.oob_lba[r] = UNMAP
            me.oob_seq[r] = 0
            me.oob_ecc[r] = 0
            me.programmed[r] = 0
            me.injected[r] = 0
            me.shadow[r] = 0xFF          # back to the erased state
            me.shadow_page[r] = emu_page_erased()
            me.par_valid[r] = 0
            var ce: i64 = 0
            while ce < CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE:
                me.oob_par[r * CODEWORDS_PER_PAGE + ce] = 0
                ce = ce + 1
            pg = pg + 1
        me.blk_erase[ne_blk] = emu_erase_counter_next(me.blk_erase[ne_blk])
        NAND_OK

    me erase_count(blk: i64) -> i64:
        """Cumulative erase count of a block (folded), clamped to a safe range."""
        if not block_in_range(blk):
            return 0
        val ne_blk: i64 = emu_fold_blk(blk, me.chip.geometry)
        if ne_blk >= me.blk_erase.len():
            return 0
        if me.blk_erase[ne_blk] < 0:
            return 0
        if me.blk_erase[ne_blk] > FW_SAFE_I64_LIMIT:
            return FW_SAFE_I64_LIMIT
        me.blk_erase[ne_blk]

    # ── fault / test hooks (same surface as fil_nand_device.NandDevice) ────────
    me inject_prog_fail(blk: i64):
        """Arm a one-shot program failure on the next program to this block."""
        if not block_in_range(blk):
            return
        val ne_blk: i64 = emu_fold_blk(blk, me.chip.geometry)
        if ne_blk < me.prog_fail.len():
            me.prog_fail[ne_blk] = 1

    me inject_erase_fail(blk: i64):
        """Arm a one-shot erase failure on the next erase of this block."""
        if not block_in_range(blk):
            return
        val ne_blk: i64 = emu_fold_blk(blk, me.chip.geometry)
        if ne_blk < me.erase_fail.len():
            me.erase_fail[ne_blk] = 1

    me inject_read_bitflip(ppn: i64, n: i64):
        """Set the reported read bit-flip count for a page (negative clamps to 0)."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, me.chip.geometry)
        if row >= me.injected.len():
            return
        if n < 0:
            me.injected[row] = 0
            return
        me.injected[row] = n

    me corrupt_page_data(ppn: i64, data: i64):
        """Overwrite the column-0 Vt cells so the page senses `data`, leaving the
        stored OOB ECC and the written-intent `shadow` untouched (mirrors
        fil_nand_device.corrupt_page_data). Because the sensed byte then diverges
        from the written intent, the next read reports the divergence as a
        bit-error count (popcount(sensed XOR intent)) — so the FIL ECC layer
        catches it via the flips path (definitively FAIL for a multi-bit corrupt),
        as well as against the stored `ecc`. No effect on an unallocated
        (never-touched) page."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return
        val geo = me.chip.geometry
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, geo)
        var c = me.chip
        var vt = c.vt
        val top: i64 = ne_cell_index(row, 0, 7, geo.page_bytes)
        if top >= vt.len():
            return
        var bit: i64 = 0
        while bit < 8:
            val idx: i64 = ne_cell_index(row, 0, bit, geo.page_bytes)
            if ((data >> bit) & 1) == 1:
                vt[idx] = ne_vt_erase_mean() as u8   # senses 1 (erased)
            else:
                vt[idx] = ne_vt_prog_mean() as u8    # senses 0 (programmed)
            bit = bit + 1
        c.vt = vt
        me.chip = c

    me set_bad(blk: i64):
        """Retire a block (subsequent program/erase to it fail closed)."""
        if not block_in_range(blk):
            return
        val ne_blk: i64 = emu_fold_blk(blk, me.chip.geometry)
        if ne_blk < me.bad_block.len():
            me.bad_block[ne_blk] = 1

    # ── NeChip test API exposed for FTL retention / SPO tests ──────────────────
    me inject(f: NeFault):
        """Arm a NeChip fault descriptor (SPO / ProgramFail / EraseFail /
        TimeAdvance). Coordinates in `f` (target_row / target_block) are CHIP
        (folded S1) coordinates; use target_row = -1 to target the next matching
        op regardless of address."""
        me.chip = me.chip.inject(f)

    me advance_time_s(seconds: f64):
        """Advance the emulator's clock (retention / disturb driver). Drift is
        applied lazily at the next sense."""
        me.chip = me.chip.advance_time_s(seconds)

    me set_vref_offset(offset: i64):
        """Shift the read reference for read-retry: vref = 128 + offset. A drooped
        (aged) programmed cell that reads 1 at the default reference reads 0 again
        once the reference is lowered."""
        me.chip = me.chip.set_vref_offset(offset)

    me vt_histogram(ppn: i64) -> [i64]:
        """Re-export the NeChip per-page stored-Vt histogram (256 bins) for soft
        observability (arch doc §3 prereq 3, §5). `ppn` is a firmware page number,
        folded into the S1 window exactly like read_page so a spec inspects the same
        cells it programmed. A half-programmed page shows two lobes (an erased lobe
        below the read reference and a programmed lobe at/above it). Read-only."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            var empty: [i64] = []
            return empty
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, me.chip.geometry)
        me.chip.vt_histogram(row)

    me read_margin(ppn: i64) -> i64:
        """Re-export the NeChip read margin (valley width in Vt codes between the
        erased and programmed lobes) for `ppn` (folded like read_page). Positive on a
        freshly-programmed page whose two lobes are cleanly separated; narrows as
        retention drift pulls the programmed lobe down toward the reference; 0 when
        the page has a single lobe (all erased or all programmed). Read-only."""
        if not ppn_in_range(ppn):
            return 0
        val row: i64 = emu_fold_row(ppn, me.chip.geometry)
        me.chip.read_margin(row)

    me set_block_wear(blk: i64, pe_cycles: i64):
        """Seed a block's program/erase-cycle count so it can be modelled as worn
        without simulating thousands of real erases. pe_cycles is the emulator's
        entire wear state (it scales retention loss), so seeding it is identical to
        having cycled the block that many times. Requires the block metadata to be
        allocated (any prior op on the chip does so)."""
        if not block_in_range(blk):
            return
        val ne_blk: i64 = emu_fold_blk(blk, me.chip.geometry)
        var c = me.chip
        if ne_blk >= c.block_metas.len():
            return
        var m = ne_block_meta_get(c.block_metas, ne_blk)
        m.pe_cycles = pe_cycles
        c.block_metas = ne_block_meta_set(c.block_metas, ne_blk, m)
        me.chip = c
